{{also|Ouch}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{unc|en}}. Some sources say the interjection is attested since 1838<ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref> (and specifically in American English) and derives ultimately from {{der|en|de|autsch}},<ref name="DC">{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref name="OED1933">{{R:New English Dictionary}} ([https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.99996/page/n521 "ouch"])</ref> perhaps specifically via {{der|en|pdc|outch||cry of pain}}, as early attestations of the interjection are from Pennsylvania.<ref name="OED1933"/> However, others say the interjection is a "mere"<ref>{{R:Century}}</ref> or "natural" exclamation attested since the mid 1600s,<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref> and the 1933 ''OED''<ref name="OED1933"/> cites one instance of a ''verb'' "ouch" in 1654, "Sancho Pancas Runs Ouching round the mountaine like a ranck-Asse".<ref>'''1654''', Gayton, ''Pleas. Notes'' IV. ii. 176, "But harke Sancho Pancas Runs Ouching round the mountaine like a ranck-Asse, Braying for's Company.</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/aʊt͡ʃ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊtʃ|s=1}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-inlandnorth-ouch.ogg|a=US,Inland North}}

====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# An expression of one's own physical [[pain]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Ouch'''! You stepped on my toe! That hurt!}}
# An expression in [[sympathy]] at another's pain.
#: {{ux|en|'''Ouch'''! Her sunburn looks awful.}}
# A reply to an [[insult]] seen as savage (frequently one that is [[tongue-in-cheek]] or joking).
#: {{ux|en|'''Ouch'''. How could you say that?}}
# An expression of disappointment.
#: {{ux|en|'''Ouch''', I really wanted to do that.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} Expressing surprise at the high [[price]] of something.
#: {{ux|en|'''Ouch''', one hundred thousand dollars for a car! I could never afford that!}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|in all of the above senses}} {{l|en|oof}}, {{l|en|ow}}, {{l|en|owie}}, {{l|en|youch}}, {{l|en|yow}}, {{l|en|yowch}}

